      "text": "1. **Identify the pattern of the ball's bounce height:**\n   The ball bounces back up to \\(\\frac{2}{3}\\) of the height from which it fell. If the initial height is \\(h_0 = 243\\) cm, then the height after the first bounce, \\(h_1\\), is:\n   \\[\n   h_1 = 243 \\times \\frac{2}{3} = 162 \\text{ cm}\n   \\]\n   The height after the second bounce, \\(h_2\\), is:\n   \\[\n   h_2 = 162 \\times \\frac{2}{3} = 108 \\text{ cm}\n   \\]\n   Following this pattern, the height after the \\(n\\)-th bounce, \\(h_n\\), is:\n   \\[\n   h_n = 243 \\times \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^n\n   \\]\n\n2. **Set up the inequality to find the number of bounces:**\n   We need to find the smallest \\(n\\) such that the height after the \\(n\\)-th bounce is less than 30 cm:\n   \\[\n   243 \\times \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^n < 30\n   \\]\n\n3. **Solve the inequality:**\n   First, isolate \\(\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^n\\) by dividing both sides by 243:\n   \\[\n   \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^n < \\frac{30}{243}\n   \\]\n   Simplify the right-hand side:\n   \\[\n   \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^n < \\frac{10}{81}\n   \\]\n   Take the natural logarithm of both sides to solve for \\(n\\):\n   \\[\n   \\ln\\left(\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^n\\right) < \\ln\\left(\\frac{10}{81}\\right)\n   \\]\n   Use the property of logarithms \\(\\ln(a^b) = b \\ln(a)\\):\n   \\[\n   n \\ln\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) < \\ln\\left(\\frac{10}{81}\\right)\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\ln\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\) is negative, divide both sides by \\(\\ln\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\) and reverse the inequality:\n   \\[\n   n > \\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{10}{81}\\right)}{\\ln\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the numerical value:**\n   Using the values of the natural logarithms:\n   \\[\n   \\ln\\left(\\frac{10}{81}\\right) \\approx -2.1972245773362196\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\ln\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) \\approx -0.4054651081081644\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   n > \\frac{-2.1972245773362196}{-0.4054651081081644} \\approx 5.42\n   \\]\n   Since \\(n\\) must be an integer, we round up to the next whole number:\n   \\[\n   n = 6\n   \\]\n\n5. **Conclusion:**\n   The ball first rises less than 30 cm after 6 bounces. Therefore, the final answer is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{6}\n   \\]",
