      "text": "If the ball first rises to $h \\le 30$ cm, then $h = \\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right)^n \\cdot 243.$ By setting $h = 10, 0, 0,$ or $-5.$ We have $\\left( \\frac{3^2}{2}\\right)^n = \\left( \\frac{9}{4} \\right)^n,$ and $(0.45)^n = 10^{-2}.$ Thus, $n = \\boxed{5}.$\n\nHere is an alternative and more visual solution.\nOn the first bounce, the height is $h = 243 \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} = 168$ cm, so the ball rises by 15 bounces to $168$ cm. After 16 bounces, the ball has risen to $168 \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} = 112$ cm. This occurs on the 17th bounce.\n\nSince $112 \\not \\le 30,$ we have that the ball first rises to less than $30$ cm on the $17^{th}$ bounce. The answer is: 17",
